1
00:00,500 --> 00:11,627
[David]: Good morning everyone. Welcome to our speech technology project review meeting. Today we are evaluating our deep learning framework for the cocktail party problem.

2
00:10,292 --> 00:18,784
[Zira]: Thanks David. I have prepared the latest evaluation benchmarks for both speaker separation and multi-noise speech enhancement.

3
00:17,765 --> 00:25,353
[David]: That is great. Can you start by summarizing the key metrics from our twenty-five multi-noise test conditions?

4
00:24,443 --> 00:34,740
[Zira]: Certainly. Across all twenty-five complex noise mixtures, our system achieved an average signal to distortion ratio gain of nearly nine decibels.

5
00:33,505 --> 00:41,133
[David]: Nine decibels is a significant gain. What about the perceptual speech quality and intelligibility scores?

6
00:40,218 --> 00:50,825
[Zira]: The PESQ score improved by one point one eight MOS, reaching two point seven nine, while the STOI intelligibility score reached zero point nine two.

7
00:49,552 --> 00:56,826
[David]: Excellent. And how does our system handle overlapping speech when two people talk at the exact same time?

8
00:55,953 --> 01:06,136
[Zira]: For overlapping speech, the scaled Conv-TasNet architecture separates the co-channel mixture in the raw time domain using dilated temporal convolutions.

9
01:04,914 --> 01:14,567
[David]: Right, and we also incorporated the mixture consistent Wiener post-filter, which successfully eliminates the high frequency metallic artifacts.

10
01:13,408 --> 01:23,671
[Zira]: Yes, exactly. The Wiener filter preserves the natural recording phase by projecting power spectral densities back onto the original mixture spectrum.

11
01:22,439 --> 01:30,828
[David]: Now let us discuss the long audio pipeline. How does the system process continuous files up to five minutes in length?

12
01:29,821 --> 01:39,978
[Zira]: We implemented a sine-windowed fifty percent overlap-add engine. It divides the audio into four-second buffers and aligns cross-chunk speaker correlations.

13
01:38,759 --> 01:46,287
[David]: That completely prevents speaker identity swapping across chunk transitions, ensuring continuous speaker tracking.

14
01:45,384 --> 01:54,132
[Zira]: Precisely. And immediately following neural separation, each isolated channel is passed to our Whisper speech-to-text engine.

15
01:53,082 --> 02:01,075
[David]: Which generates time-synchronized, speaker-attributed dialogue transcripts and exportable SubRip subtitle files.

16
02:00,116 --> 02:10,104
[Zira]: In addition, we deployed the new React web interface with FastAPI backend, achieving real-time factors below zero point zero five on the GPU.

17
02:08,905 --> 02:15,034
[David]: That is over twenty times faster than real time on our NVIDIA TITAN Xp graphics card.

18
02:14,298 --> 02:23,611
[Zira]: Yes, the user can now upload any audio file, view instant waveform scrubbing, and download the clean separated speech streams.

19
02:22,494 --> 02:31,661
[David]: This will have a profound societal impact on telemedicine consultations, courtroom audio forensics, and assistive hearing technology.

20
02:30,561 --> 02:39,524
[Zira]: I agree completely. All models, checkpoints, and documentation are now finalized and ready for the technical project defense.

21
02:38,449 --> 02:45,662
[David]: Thank you for the comprehensive update. Let us conclude this session and proceed with the live demonstration.

